# Odoo 19 Development Standards

This project targets **Odoo 19 exclusively**.

All generated, suggested, completed, modified, reviewed, or refactored code
must be compatible with Odoo 19.

## Odoo version

- Use Odoo 19 APIs, syntax, conventions, and recommended patterns.
- Do not use deprecated APIs, attributes, XML syntax, ORM patterns, or
  conventions from previous Odoo versions when an Odoo 19 alternative exists.
- Never assume that code valid in Odoo 16, 17, or 18 is valid in Odoo 19.
- Do not introduce compatibility code for older Odoo versions unless
  explicitly requested.

## Python and ORM

- Follow Odoo 19 ORM conventions and prefer idiomatic Odoo code.
- Use the Odoo ORM instead of raw SQL whenever reasonably possible.
- Write recordset-aware code.
- Avoid singleton assumptions unless the operation explicitly requires one.
- Avoid unnecessary database queries and inefficient ORM patterns.
- Use appropriate Odoo decorators and method signatures for Odoo 19.
- Follow Odoo 19 conventions for fields, relational fields, computed fields,
  related fields, constraints, onchange methods, and ORM method overrides.
- Prefer clear and maintainable implementations over unnecessary abstractions.

## XML and UI

- Use Odoo 19 XML view syntax and conventions.
- Do not use XML attributes, view patterns, or mechanisms deprecated or
  removed in Odoo 19.
- Follow Odoo 19 conventions for views, actions, menus, templates, and assets.
- Use current Odoo 19 frontend conventions when working with JavaScript,
  OWL, QWeb, or web assets.

## Security

- Follow Odoo 19 security conventions.
- Correctly distinguish between groups, access control lists (ACLs),
  and record rules.
- Do not bypass Odoo security mechanisms unless explicitly required and
  justified.
- Avoid unnecessary use of sudo().

## Module structure

- Follow standard Odoo 19 module structure and manifest conventions.
- Keep models, views, security, data, controllers, tests, and frontend code
  organized according to Odoo conventions.
- Reuse existing models, fields, methods, XML IDs, and framework mechanisms
  when appropriate.
- Do not invent model names, field names, XML IDs, methods, APIs, or framework
  behavior when their existence is uncertain.

## Code quality

- Prefer simple, explicit, readable, and maintainable code.
- Follow Python and Odoo naming conventions.
- Avoid unnecessary complexity, premature abstractions, and redundant code.
- When several valid implementations exist, prefer the simplest idiomatic
  Odoo 19 solution.
- When modifying existing code, preserve the project's established conventions
  when they are compatible with Odoo 19.

## Verification and context

- Use existing knowledge and the currently available project context by default.
- Do not routinely search documentation, inspect the Odoo source tree, or
  explore unrelated project files before generating code.
- Do not perform broad searches merely to confirm standard Odoo behavior.
- If an Odoo 19-specific API, syntax, or behavior is genuinely uncertain,
  acknowledge the uncertainty rather than silently using an older-version
  pattern.
- When verification is genuinely necessary, inspect only the specific relevant
  symbol, file, or documentation needed to resolve that uncertainty.

## Learning-oriented development

This is also a learning project.

- Prefer code whose behavior and Odoo mechanisms are understandable.
- Do not introduce advanced abstractions or unnecessary complexity when a
  straightforward Odoo 19 implementation is sufficient.
- When explaining generated code, use precise Odoo terminology and clearly
  distinguish Python behavior from Odoo ORM/framework behavior.